1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:02,060
Make sure your trays are in their upright position.

2
00:00:02,740 --> 00:00:06,340
And prepare yourselves for a journey through the history of airfields.

3
00:00:06,760 --> 00:00:12,460
From primitive farmers' fields, through the constantly evolving and expanding multi-mile layouts of today.

4
00:00:13,380 --> 00:00:16,460
Now, International Airports on Modern Marbles.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
You probably think keeping a 430-ton airplane flying is just a physics problem.

6
00:00:46,220 --> 00:00:47,220
Think again.

7
00:00:48,020 --> 00:00:51,060
The real challenge starts when the plane's back on the ground.

8
00:00:52,680 --> 00:00:58,240
Consider for a moment the difficulties facing modern airports as they link and shrink our busy world.

9
00:00:58,241 --> 00:01:02,240
They are models of intricately coordinated organization.

10
00:01:03,400 --> 00:01:06,940
Symphonies of managed chaos and unexpected change.

11
00:01:07,580 --> 00:01:10,440
As they send more people further, faster.

12
00:01:11,420 --> 00:01:17,140
Over 72,000 of us hurl ourselves into the air on thousands of planes every hour of every day.

13
00:01:17,740 --> 00:01:24,680
And safely land anywhere, from Anchorage to Zurich, in less time than it takes to read the Sunday newspaper.

14
00:01:24,681 --> 00:01:31,680
Fifty years ago, it took more than a day to fly from Los Angeles to Japan.

15
00:01:31,681 --> 00:01:38,680
Today, a flight from LAX to the new Kansai International Airport takes less than 14 hours.

16
00:01:38,681 --> 00:01:43,680
Kansai features the latest trend in airport construction.

17
00:01:43,681 --> 00:01:50,680
It is built on a 5.6 million square foot man-made island in the middle of the ocean.

18
00:01:50,681 --> 00:02:01,675
Kansai is a response to the overwhelming need for more airport services in a place where encroaching urban sprawl and a lack of available land make a big noisy

19
00:02:01,676 --> 00:02:03,680
downtown airport undesirable.

20
00:02:03,681 --> 00:02:11,560
It's a brilliant concept to look at.

21
00:02:11,561 --> 00:02:17,600
We think, well, wouldn't we rather land closer to the action, closer to where we really want

22
00:02:17,601 --> 00:02:18,600
to be?

23
00:02:18,601 --> 00:02:23,220
The truth of the matter is we want to land where we can get in and out and get the minimum

24
00:02:23,221 --> 00:02:26,880
hassle in terms of being able to move through the facilities.

25
00:02:26,881 --> 00:02:31,200
And Kansai certainly offers that.

26
00:02:31,201 --> 00:02:36,680
spent two years reclaiming land from the sea as a foundation for the airport, filling a

27
00:02:36,681 --> 00:02:42,800
massive rectangular seawall with 6,357 million cubic feet of earth.

28
00:02:46,820 --> 00:02:53,245
They built a 2.3-mile-long two-tiered access bridge for trains and cars.

29
00:02:53,246 --> 00:02:54,940
Plus, they included

30
00:02:54,941 --> 00:03:01,980
a hotel, shopping complex, observation deck, pedestrian walkways, administration buildings,

31
00:03:02,780 --> 00:03:07,400
street lamps, dozens of restrooms, and everything else you'd find in a small city.

32
00:03:09,560 --> 00:03:17,480
First conceived back in 1968, the facility finally opened in 1994 as Japan's first 24-hour

33
00:03:17,481 --> 00:03:25,540
Airport, operating a 2.1 mile long runway and an average of 319 flights per day.

34
00:03:27,220 --> 00:03:32,600
The usual array of security measures, gates and ticketing counters are housed in a terminal

35
00:03:32,601 --> 00:03:35,860
designed to reflect the culture and architecture of the region.

36
00:03:37,360 --> 00:03:42,820
The concept of building offshore airports and airports in locations where you otherwise

37
00:03:42,821 --> 00:03:46,560
might not have expected them are becoming more and more the vogue.

38
00:03:47,480 --> 00:03:52,480
There are risks, of course, to building an airport at sea.

39
00:03:52,481 --> 00:03:55,480
Engineers expected Kansai to settle a little.

40
00:03:55,481 --> 00:03:59,480
Unfortunately, it is settling faster than engineers expected,

41
00:03:59,481 --> 00:04:02,480
at a rate of nearly four feet per year,

42
00:04:02,481 --> 00:04:05,480
and officials are scrambling for a solution.

43
00:04:07,480 --> 00:04:10,480
It hasn't been a smooth ride to Kansai from Kitty Hawk,

44
00:04:10,481 --> 00:04:13,480
but it has been an exciting evolution.

45
00:04:13,481 --> 00:04:19,560
Aviation and airports have grown together into multi-billion dollar industries

46
00:04:19,561 --> 00:04:22,800
that do far more than provide planes with places to land.

47
00:04:22,801 --> 00:04:26,200
But that's how they began at the turn of the last century.

48
00:04:26,201 --> 00:04:29,560
The initial airports were

49
00:04:29,561 --> 00:04:33,760
little more than afterthoughts. They were grass fields that were out in the

50
00:04:33,761 --> 00:04:34,461
middle of

51
00:04:34,462 --> 00:04:37,800
farmers pasture. Little reception areas for

52
00:04:37,801 --> 00:04:43,280
passengers. Farmers pastures were good locations because they were flat,

53
00:04:43,281 --> 00:04:47,440
clear and the farmer usually had reliable weather information for the pilots.

54
00:04:50,320 --> 00:04:55,520
Partly as a byproduct of the First World War, air travel caught on more quickly in Europe

55
00:04:55,521 --> 00:04:57,669
than in the United States.

56
00:04:57,670 --> 00:05:02,960
Roads and railways had been destroyed and often the easiest path

57
00:05:02,961 --> 00:05:10,940
from one country to another was by air. The war also produced experienced pilots.

58
00:05:10,941 --> 00:05:17,940
European governments subsidized airports in London, Paris and Berlin, where large facilities

59
00:05:18,220 --> 00:05:23,244
were modeled on the grand train stations of the 19th century.

60
00:05:23,245 --> 00:05:25,220
Passengers merely walked

61
00:05:25,420 --> 00:05:30,526
from a large foyer out to a single gate or onto an open field.

62
00:05:30,527 --> 00:05:32,420
In America, a spectator

63
00:05:32,820 --> 00:05:37,900
sport full of daredevils would be transformed by the post office into a lucrative courier

64
00:05:37,901 --> 00:05:39,706
service for the mail.

65
00:05:39,707 --> 00:05:45,900
The airlines were getting a subsidy to carry mail in their very earliest

66
00:05:45,901 --> 00:05:46,601
stages.

67
00:05:46,602 --> 00:05:50,640
As a matter of fact, they really didn't want to carry passengers because they didn't

68
00:05:50,641 --> 00:05:55,691
have to feed the mail. They just put it on the plane. It was a simple process.

69
00:05:55,692 --> 00:05:56,392
It was

70
00:05:56,393 --> 00:06:01,760
also a deadly one as ill-equipped pilots flew unscheduled routes through unpredictable weather.

71
00:06:01,761 --> 00:06:07,740
31 of the first 40 pilots hired by the Postal Service in 1919

72
00:06:07,741 --> 00:06:10,740
died in crashes within six years

73
00:06:10,741 --> 00:06:15,580
in response the Postal Service opened their airmail operation

74
00:06:15,581 --> 00:06:18,760
to private contractors who bid on specific routes

75
00:06:18,761 --> 00:06:24,100
but that would all change with Walter Folger Brown's appointment

76
00:06:24,101 --> 00:06:26,640
as Postmaster General in 1929

77
00:06:26,641 --> 00:06:31,180
Brown encouraged commercial aviation by forcing ragtag

78
00:06:31,181 --> 00:06:35,020
independent airlines to merge into a handful of large ones.

79
00:06:35,980 --> 00:06:41,260
When he took over, there were about 42 little airlines in the United States.

80
00:06:41,500 --> 00:06:43,860
He said, we want a proper national system.

81
00:06:45,600 --> 00:06:50,300
In 1927, the aviation industry was bolstered by a public relations event

82
00:06:50,301 --> 00:06:53,440
almost as monumental as the discovery of flight itself.

83
00:06:55,660 --> 00:06:57,960
This is Law Thomas in New York.

84
00:06:58,580 --> 00:06:59,300
He made it.

85
00:06:59,301 --> 00:07:00,056
Charles A.

86
00:07:00,057 --> 00:07:07,540
Lindbergh, Lucky Lindy as they call him, landed at Le Bourget Airport Paris for 524 this afternoon.

87
00:07:08,480 --> 00:07:15,433
Lindbergh became the first person to fly solo non-stop across the Atlantic, landing in Paris in his spirit of St.

88
00:07:15,434 --> 00:07:16,540
Louis on May 21st,

89
00:07:17,220 --> 00:07:20,080
thus opening the age of transatlantic international air travel.

90
00:07:20,600 --> 00:07:27,260
What Charles Lindbergh did for commercial aviation has been, to a large extent, overlooked.

91
00:07:27,960 --> 00:07:30,520
He'd take his spirit of St. Louis into a town.

92
00:07:31,260 --> 00:07:33,600
He'd land at the farmer's field at the edge of town,

93
00:07:33,920 --> 00:07:36,180
be escorted to the steps of City Hall.

94
00:07:36,740 --> 00:07:39,800
The mayor would come out and hand Charles Lindbergh a key to the city,

95
00:07:39,801 --> 00:07:43,780
and in most cases, a week, a month later,

96
00:07:44,060 --> 00:07:47,380
the city would float a bond for a half a million dollars and build an airport.

97
00:07:48,520 --> 00:07:50,540
But what exactly was an airport?

98
00:07:51,440 --> 00:07:54,240
Was it a kind of train station? An open field?

99
00:07:54,241 --> 00:08:05,455
As land became more scarce and more costly, it became obvious that you couldn't have these very large land sections dedicated strictly for an all-field type of

100
00:08:05,456 --> 00:08:10,040
airport, and so therefore the whole runway concept was developed.

101
00:08:10,580 --> 00:08:12,340
But where to put the runway?

102
00:08:13,240 --> 00:08:19,620
In 1929, the Lehigh Portland Cement Company sponsored a competition that produced some curious results.

103
00:08:19,621 --> 00:08:26,520
One submission had airplanes landing on runways suspended atop city skyscrapers.

104
00:08:27,740 --> 00:08:30,960
They loved the idea of gridding out patterns.

105
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:37,420
And so there's aesthetically wonderful patterns, stars, spokes.

106
00:08:37,760 --> 00:08:42,520
The designs that appeared in this airport competition are done by architects

107
00:08:42,521 --> 00:08:45,960
for the most part who had no experience in airport design

108
00:08:45,961 --> 00:08:49,340
and also never seem to have been hired since.

109
00:08:49,620 --> 00:08:54,620
Landing airplanes on top of skyscrapers may have been far-fetched,

110
00:08:55,120 --> 00:08:58,080
but landing them on water soon became commonplace.

111
00:08:59,220 --> 00:09:02,760
During the early 1930s, the epitome of passenger plane travel

112
00:09:02,761 --> 00:09:05,120
was the luxurious flying boat,

113
00:09:05,760 --> 00:09:09,280
an innovative airplane that used its smooth, reinforced underbelly

114
00:09:09,281 --> 00:09:10,620
to take off and land.

115
00:09:12,660 --> 00:09:16,940
All one needed was a few hundred feet of calm seas and a dock.

116
00:09:16,941 --> 00:09:23,656
In fact, the very first passenger airline actually started as a seaplane service in St.

117
00:09:23,657 --> 00:09:25,200
Petersburg, Florida.

118
00:09:25,960 --> 00:09:29,220
And in the early days, the seaplanes were very popular.

119
00:09:30,900 --> 00:09:35,400
Miami's Dinner Key became America's very first international airport,

120
00:09:36,060 --> 00:09:40,500
offering trips to southern international destinations on Pan American Airlines.

121
00:09:42,540 --> 00:09:46,640
In fact, the original terminal at Dinner Key was a houseboat.

122
00:09:46,940 --> 00:09:51,940
One prominent visitor to Miami's Dinner Quay was New York's Mayor Fiorello LaGuardia,

123
00:09:51,941 --> 00:09:57,940
who wouldn't be satisfied until his city built the greatest airport in the world.

124
00:10:00,940 --> 00:10:05,940
International passenger service was burgeoning in Europe between the wars.

125
00:10:05,941 --> 00:10:14,940
In Hamburg, Germany, for instance, an innovative brick terminal was handling 62 passengers a day when it was completed in 1929.

126
00:10:16,940 --> 00:10:24,080
Efficiency and organization became a keystone as architects built new airports in places like Germany, France and England.

127
00:10:27,300 --> 00:10:35,900
This attention to efficient traffic flow was apparent at London's Gatwick Airport, which opened for international passenger service in 1936.

128
00:10:35,901 --> 00:10:44,220
The lovely thing about Gatwick, and it should be pointed out that the original Gatwick terminal was a modern miracle,

129
00:10:44,700 --> 00:10:49,120
in that it didn't take a long distance to get from the front door to the airplane,

130
00:10:49,700 --> 00:10:53,860
that it had a circular design, much like a beehive at the end of a finger,

131
00:10:54,020 --> 00:10:56,420
and it had covered walkways out to the airplanes.

132
00:10:56,920 --> 00:11:01,260
It had many of the elements that you see today in a modern airport.

133
00:11:01,261 --> 00:11:07,080
The Beehive terminal allowed a plane landing at Gatwick's open field runway

134
00:11:07,081 --> 00:11:10,820
to park at one of many gates rather than one or two gates

135
00:11:10,821 --> 00:11:13,860
that handled Gatwick's roughly a dozen daily flights

136
00:11:13,861 --> 00:11:18,340
It also included telescoping canopies

137
00:11:18,341 --> 00:11:21,640
that could protrude from the terminal out to a loading airplane

138
00:11:21,641 --> 00:11:23,280
like a spoke on a wheel

139
00:11:23,281 --> 00:11:28,200
This innovation kept passengers protected from the elements

140
00:11:28,201 --> 00:11:31,080
evolving into the loading ramps used today

141
00:11:31,081 --> 00:11:36,080
Access to the Beehive was through an underground walkway.

142
00:11:36,081 --> 00:11:41,080
Critics complained that the circular gate left no room for expansion.

143
00:11:41,081 --> 00:11:46,080
But in fact, the design allowed for multiple beehives throughout the airport.

144
00:11:46,081 --> 00:11:55,080
In America, New Jersey's Newark Airport was the world's busiest in 1935, with 30 flights a day.

145
00:11:55,081 --> 00:12:00,080
Handling only domestic flights, Newark was not an international airport.

146
00:12:00,081 --> 00:12:03,663
airport. But it inadvertently inspired one.

147
00:12:03,664 --> 00:12:07,080
Newark Airport was the aerial gateway for

148
00:12:07,520 --> 00:12:08,344
New York City.

149
00:12:08,345 --> 00:12:12,520
In fact, Mayor LaGuardia had a flight on American Airlines from Chicago

150
00:12:12,521 --> 00:12:18,260
in 1937 and he landed in Newark and he told the pilot, no, my ticket says New York and

151
00:12:18,261 --> 00:12:21,828
I want you to take me there. So they took off from Newark and flew.

152
00:12:21,829 --> 00:12:22,840
It was a press gag,

153
00:12:22,841 --> 00:12:28,200
of course, but they flew to Floyd Bennett Field. Floyd Bennett Field was New York's

154
00:12:28,201 --> 00:12:32,100
official airport but it was further from Manhattan the Newark was

155
00:12:32,101 --> 00:12:35,860
so the Postal Service used Newark for its airmail into New York

156
00:12:35,861 --> 00:12:41,100
LaGuardia wouldn't stand for that make no mistake about it

157
00:12:41,101 --> 00:12:45,320
this airport was not going to be denied mayor LaGuardia was gonna build this

158
00:12:45,321 --> 00:12:47,320
airport and there was no question about it

159
00:12:47,321 --> 00:12:52,160
LaGuardia hired the architects who designed Dinner Key in Florida

160
00:12:52,161 --> 00:12:57,140
the Art Deco flavor of that earlier airport would be repeated in New York's

161
00:12:57,141 --> 00:13:01,140
new municipal airport eight miles from the city.

162
00:13:01,141 --> 00:13:04,520
The circular marine air terminal was positioned on the shore to handle the

163
00:13:04,521 --> 00:13:07,900
luxurious seaplane flights.

164
00:13:07,901 --> 00:13:11,240
The planes would float over to the back of the terminal dock by piers that

165
00:13:11,241 --> 00:13:15,300
provided walkways inside the rotunda.

166
00:13:15,301 --> 00:13:19,420
The airport also provided international travel via seaplane

167
00:13:19,421 --> 00:13:24,260
as land-based passenger planes could not yet carry passengers overseas.

168
00:13:24,261 --> 00:13:29,920
The seaplanes traveled east to Europe with fueling stops at coastal ports in Canada, Greenland and Iceland.

169
00:13:31,660 --> 00:13:36,320
New York City not only had a world-class airport, it had an international one.

170
00:13:37,660 --> 00:13:44,460
LaGuardia opened up the entire European landscape to scheduled air flights,

171
00:13:44,461 --> 00:13:49,200
as well as the domestic landscape to scheduled flights into New York.

172
00:13:50,400 --> 00:13:54,200
The airport also had a terminal for land-based domestic flights.

173
00:13:54,260 --> 00:13:59,260
Significantly, travelers at the airport were divided for greater efficiency.

174
00:13:59,261 --> 00:14:04,260
Departing passengers were upstairs. Arrival passengers came downstairs.

175
00:14:04,261 --> 00:14:08,260
There was a clear definition between arrival and departing passengers.

176
00:14:08,261 --> 00:14:15,260
And of course on the departure level were all the great services, the beautiful restaurant, the open terrace they called it.

177
00:14:15,261 --> 00:14:17,260
It was a lovely affair.

178
00:14:17,261 --> 00:14:23,460
New York Municipal Airport boasted two nearly mile-long runways,

179
00:14:23,920 --> 00:14:27,540
handling 250 flights its first summer in 1939.

180
00:14:29,180 --> 00:14:34,000
On opening day, a group of Skyriders flew above the crowds and memorably wrote,

181
00:14:34,580 --> 00:14:35,640
Name it LaGuardia.

182
00:14:36,300 --> 00:14:40,180
It took the city council a couple of years to rename the airport LaGuardia,

183
00:14:40,680 --> 00:14:43,160
but it became a hotspot soon after it opened.

184
00:14:43,161 --> 00:14:49,160
The observation decks were busy day and night watching the airplanes come and go.

185
00:14:49,161 --> 00:14:51,160
Barbershops were here in the 30s.

186
00:14:51,161 --> 00:14:58,160
There were small coffee shops and intimate cozy little areas at the airport to sit and have a quick drink before a flight.

187
00:14:58,161 --> 00:15:03,160
What the mayor and others who created LaGuardia Airport couldn't have anticipated

188
00:15:03,161 --> 00:15:08,160
was the rapid expansion of passenger airplane travel over the next decade.

189
00:15:08,161 --> 00:15:15,320
Air travel demand greatly increased, especially after World War II, and LaGuardia was clearly

190
00:15:15,321 --> 00:15:21,080
inadequate to handle the volume of flights, both domestic and international.

191
00:15:21,081 --> 00:15:27,040
New York needed another airport.

192
00:15:27,041 --> 00:15:32,000
World War II had stimulated the development of larger, faster, more efficient aircraft

193
00:15:32,001 --> 00:15:35,280
that could also travel further.

194
00:15:35,281 --> 00:15:41,400
Redesigned after the war, these new multi-engine planes not only carried more passengers on longer trips,

195
00:15:42,140 --> 00:15:48,040
they sounded the death knell of the elegant but inefficient flying boats, which carried only about a dozen passengers.

196
00:15:49,980 --> 00:15:53,620
These new aircraft demanded more from the airports that serviced them.

197
00:15:54,160 --> 00:15:59,860
They sacrificed the ability to take off quickly in a short space.

198
00:15:59,861 --> 00:16:03,120
but by having longer runways

199
00:16:03,121 --> 00:16:06,600
and getting up at much higher speed to take off

200
00:16:06,601 --> 00:16:08,360
they could carry heavier loads

201
00:16:08,361 --> 00:16:12,540
and the length of runways began to grow

202
00:16:12,541 --> 00:16:15,120
and by the end of the Second World War

203
00:16:15,121 --> 00:16:17,760
you had to have at least a mile of runway

204
00:16:17,761 --> 00:16:20,100
if you were going to hope to be recognised

205
00:16:20,101 --> 00:16:22,760
as a class airfield.

206
00:16:24,780 --> 00:16:28,040
Airports in Europe also expanded to accommodate the new planes.

207
00:16:29,040 --> 00:16:32,400
Amsterdam's 30-year-old Schiphol International Airport, for instance,

208
00:16:32,780 --> 00:16:38,940
was destroyed during the war but rebuilt and reopened in 1946, ready for the larger planes.

209
00:16:40,880 --> 00:16:49,380
In addition to refurbished airports, brand-new airports, like England's Heathrow, 15 miles from London, met the international demand.

210
00:16:50,620 --> 00:16:56,560
Within its first year of operation, airport planners speculated building nine runways to handle all the traffic.

211
00:16:57,400 --> 00:16:59,000
They ultimately built three.

212
00:17:01,020 --> 00:17:03,200
While Heathrow was being built overseas,

213
00:17:03,940 --> 00:17:07,580
in America, New York International Airport opened in 1948,

214
00:17:08,220 --> 00:17:12,420
in Jamaica Bay at the southeast end of Long Island, 15 miles from the city.

215
00:17:13,700 --> 00:17:17,920
Later renamed John F. Kennedy International Airport, it was enormous.

216
00:17:19,040 --> 00:17:21,520
Well, you could take all of LaGuardia Airport

217
00:17:21,521 --> 00:17:26,220
and fit it very neatly inside the passenger terminal oval.

218
00:17:26,560 --> 00:17:30,987
at Kennedy. There's 600 acres here, there's 5,000 acres there.

219
00:17:30,988 --> 00:17:32,700
So there was the feeling

220
00:17:32,701 --> 00:17:35,060
that there was an endless amount of room to grow.

221
00:17:37,080 --> 00:17:41,985
But runway length was not the only difference between LaGuardia and JFK.

222
00:17:41,986 --> 00:17:43,280
Instead of a single

223
00:17:43,281 --> 00:17:50,040
passenger terminal serving a host of airlines, JFK pioneered the airport city concept, with

224
00:17:50,041 --> 00:17:52,525
each airplane building its own terminal.

225
00:17:52,526 --> 00:17:55,320
It also had its own police department and its

226
00:17:55,321 --> 00:17:57,108
electrical power plants.

227
00:17:57,109 --> 00:18:01,800
The most interesting thing about JFK International Airport was,

228
00:18:01,801 --> 00:18:08,276
here was the ultimate expression of the airlines versus the railroads and the steamships.

229
00:18:08,277 --> 00:18:08,977
Every

230
00:18:08,978 --> 00:18:12,827
one of the carriers went out to that field and built their own statement.

231
00:18:12,828 --> 00:18:13,860
The buildings were

232
00:18:13,861 --> 00:18:20,673
designed with flair and a sense of destination and a sense of excitement.

233
00:18:20,674 --> 00:18:22,540
The 150-foot control

234
00:18:22,541 --> 00:18:29,500
tower completed in 1952 was one of the earliest freestanding control towers, coordinating

235
00:18:29,501 --> 00:18:31,868
roughly 50 flights a day.

236
00:18:31,869 --> 00:18:37,740
JFK was America's busiest international airport throughout the

237
00:18:37,741 --> 00:18:44,700
1950s, but the introduction of passenger jet aircraft in 1958 meant bigger planes and more

238
00:18:44,701 --> 00:18:51,700
flights, airports would have to adjust to the jet age.

239
00:18:51,701 --> 00:18:57,160
In 1946, the world's busiest airport was Amsterdam's Schiphol International Airport.

240
00:18:57,161 --> 00:19:06,660
With 350,000 passengers a year, international airports will return on Modern Marvels.

241
00:19:06,661 --> 00:19:12,340
The technology of flight advanced yet again in the 1950s, when passenger jets sent a shock

242
00:19:12,341 --> 00:19:16,340
through the community of airport architects.

243
00:19:16,341 --> 00:19:24,340
Bigger, faster, heavier planes required leaner, cleaner, more efficient jet-age airports.

244
00:19:24,341 --> 00:19:30,340
Jet aircraft technology allowed the aircraft to become larger and larger

245
00:19:30,341 --> 00:19:33,340
because of the thrust-to-weight ratios.

246
00:19:33,341 --> 00:19:38,340
And as the aircraft got larger, being able to get the aircraft into a gate

247
00:19:38,341 --> 00:19:43,120
without touching wings with an airplane that might be sitting in the next gate over,

248
00:19:43,560 --> 00:19:44,920
became a real problem.

249
00:19:47,660 --> 00:19:49,160
So did the noise.

250
00:19:50,160 --> 00:19:51,600
Jets were louder than propellers.

251
00:19:52,420 --> 00:19:54,700
Airports needed more room to land the bigger planes,

252
00:19:55,040 --> 00:19:58,180
and they needed to be far enough from the city to avoid noise pollution.

253
00:19:59,380 --> 00:20:02,280
Yet they had to be close enough to be convenient.

254
00:20:02,281 --> 00:20:09,080
Larger, more efficient planes meant more cost-efficient flights.

255
00:20:09,081 --> 00:20:13,040
As a result, air travel opened up to even more consumers.

256
00:20:13,041 --> 00:20:19,520
The success of the jet is not so much the speed that it offered, but that it reduced

257
00:20:19,521 --> 00:20:21,280
costs so dramatically.

258
00:20:21,281 --> 00:20:29,220
And when the seat mile costs go down, then that enables a volume of traffic and invites

259
00:20:29,221 --> 00:20:30,220
a new community.

260
00:20:30,221 --> 00:20:41,400
Commercial jet service began at London's Heathrow in 1952, six years before any other international airport, with flights to Johannesburg, South Africa.

261
00:20:42,400 --> 00:20:48,840
In 1953, passengers through Heathrow topped the million mark, the first airport to do so.

262
00:20:49,660 --> 00:20:52,280
Three years later, Heathrow saw three million.

263
00:20:52,281 --> 00:20:58,460
The first international airport in America built specifically for the jet age was Washington's

264
00:20:58,461 --> 00:21:10,280
Dulles, opened in 1962 and designed by Aero Saarinen.

265
00:21:10,281 --> 00:21:15,320
Three runways at Dulles were constructed of reinforced poured concrete, each over two

266
00:21:15,321 --> 00:21:21,320
miles long.

267
00:21:21,321 --> 00:21:25,940
A modern air traffic control tower was constructed to a height of 177 feet,

268
00:21:26,500 --> 00:21:28,800
27 feet higher than JFK's tower.

269
00:21:32,580 --> 00:21:36,860
Dulles also pioneered the use of traveling lounges, or people movers,

270
00:21:37,040 --> 00:21:39,460
to ferry passengers more efficiently to their gates.

271
00:21:41,280 --> 00:21:45,580
Instead of trying to put a lot of people in a gate situation and jam them in,

272
00:21:45,581 --> 00:21:49,560
you could move people onto these people movers that would take them out to the aircraft,

273
00:21:49,561 --> 00:21:52,000
waiting to board to take off.

274
00:21:52,280 --> 00:21:54,800
So it attempted several different technologies

275
00:21:54,801 --> 00:21:57,280
which have become, in one way or another,

276
00:21:57,400 --> 00:21:59,820
commonplace at airports all over the world.

277
00:22:02,020 --> 00:22:05,280
Situated more than 25 miles from Washington, D.C.,

278
00:22:05,281 --> 00:22:06,620
on 10,000 acres,

279
00:22:07,340 --> 00:22:09,340
the airport was planned for rural Virginia

280
00:22:09,341 --> 00:22:10,740
to reduce noise pollution

281
00:22:10,741 --> 00:22:13,500
and provide the extra room required by jets.

282
00:22:15,700 --> 00:22:18,700
However, communities soon sprouted up near Dulles,

283
00:22:18,701 --> 00:22:20,540
as they often do around airports,

284
00:22:20,980 --> 00:22:25,200
to take advantage of opportunities the dramatic new airport would inevitably generate.

285
00:22:28,640 --> 00:22:34,140
Saarinen's startling design included a roofline that swept dramatically up in a metaphor of flight.

286
00:22:35,720 --> 00:22:39,140
You want to have sort of that aerodynamic flow, if you will.

287
00:22:39,141 --> 00:22:42,380
Lots of light, lots of open space,

288
00:22:43,120 --> 00:22:47,860
sort of the embodiment, again, of blue skies and the sky's the limit.

289
00:22:48,700 --> 00:22:56,040
The terminal at Dulles International Airport was undeniably, unequivocally, unabashedly an airport.

290
00:22:58,360 --> 00:23:06,640
As Dulles was making an architectural impression in the east, another jet-age airport in the west was doing its best to keep a low profile.

291
00:23:08,480 --> 00:23:17,100
Los Angeles International Airport, or LAX, began in the teens as an airfield in the bean fields 18 miles west of downtown LA,

292
00:23:17,101 --> 00:23:19,380
but quickly grew after the war.

293
00:23:21,700 --> 00:23:25,100
Expanding into an international airport in the late 1950s,

294
00:23:25,560 --> 00:23:28,640
it took a radically different approach to architecture than Dulles.

295
00:23:29,160 --> 00:23:34,480
I've often referred to this airport as an airport without any exterior architecture.

296
00:23:35,040 --> 00:23:39,200
When you drive in, the buildings on either side are one and two stories,

297
00:23:39,400 --> 00:23:43,100
and you see a road and you see glass fronts and you don't see any structures.

298
00:23:43,101 --> 00:23:52,700
With the strong Los Angeles car culture, the architects anticipated lots of vehicles coming to the airport and designed accordingly.

299
00:23:54,340 --> 00:24:01,760
Traffic is mitigated by LAX's innovative horseshoe-designed roadway that passes separate terminals aligned in a row.

300
00:24:01,761 --> 00:24:14,413
The reason for this in Los Angeles is that we did not want to make the passenger walk further than 40 feet from where he unloaded his baggage at the curb to the face

301
00:24:14,414 --> 00:24:16,100
of the ticket counter.

302
00:24:17,800 --> 00:24:24,420
Within the Horseshoe Roadway were parking lots, which could and would be expanded into multi-level parking garages.

303
00:24:25,400 --> 00:24:30,880
The design was so effective at controlling traffic that it took some observers by surprise.

304
00:24:31,760 --> 00:24:44,848
When we opened in 1961, the LA Times especially came out and photographed the empty streets and talked about the fact that we'd overspent the $20 million for the

305
00:24:44,849 --> 00:24:45,580
terminal.

306
00:24:46,360 --> 00:24:53,160
In the ensuing 20 years, population and travel went up, airplanes got bigger, and more people were traveling.

307
00:24:53,780 --> 00:24:57,240
Then in 1982, it was we built the second level.

308
00:24:57,241 --> 00:25:04,240
Los Angeles improved on the old LaGuardia concept of dividing, arriving and departing travelers

309
00:25:04,241 --> 00:25:07,240
by separating them before they even got out of their cars.

310
00:25:07,241 --> 00:25:12,240
At LAX, departing travelers are sent to the upper level roadway

311
00:25:12,241 --> 00:25:14,240
and arrivals to the lower roadway.

312
00:25:14,241 --> 00:25:19,240
Observers were once again surprised at the success of the scheme.

313
00:25:19,241 --> 00:25:25,240
So when we opened the new second level roadway,

314
00:25:25,241 --> 00:25:31,500
they were out again with the mobile units but the story was that gee we're sorry folks we're not

315
00:25:31,501 --> 00:25:35,620
going to be able to come out here anymore and report on the traffic jail because there aren't

316
00:25:35,621 --> 00:25:41,720
any but that was almost 20 years ago now and we're back to the point where traffic is overloading the

317
00:25:41,721 --> 00:25:43,400
second level roadway.

318
00:25:43,401 --> 00:25:49,240
LAX was one of the first airports to have the fueling hydrants built into

319
00:25:49,241 --> 00:25:55,420
the aprons of the tarmac to facilitate underwing fueling, thus avoiding expensive collisions

320
00:25:55,421 --> 00:26:03,200
between jet wings and fueling carts. LAX incorporated a cutting-edge baggage claim

321
00:26:03,201 --> 00:26:06,214
system built around the new carousel design.

322
00:26:06,215 --> 00:26:09,160
Luggage could now be loaded onto a conveyor

323
00:26:09,161 --> 00:26:14,060
belt that whisked it to a retrieval area, often arriving there before its owner did.

324
00:26:14,061 --> 00:26:21,380
A state-of-the-art control tower, 13 stories high, set on top of the airport administration

325
00:26:21,381 --> 00:26:28,680
building, handling nearly 1,500 flights a day and carrying roughly 33,000 people on

326
00:26:28,681 --> 00:26:33,000
three runways, each roughly two miles long.

327
00:26:33,001 --> 00:26:35,220
The sheer size of the equipment was daunting.

328
00:26:35,221 --> 00:26:40,460
Originally, they had three levels of nothing but electronics.

329
00:26:40,461 --> 00:26:44,700
through the years of the electronics improvements came along they just

330
00:26:44,701 --> 00:26:50,080
reduced the size of the equipment down to where they didn't need all that space

331
00:26:50,081 --> 00:26:53,340
after constructing a clean efficient subtle airport

332
00:26:53,341 --> 00:26:58,760
the architects at LAX then planted an outlandish landmark smack in its center

333
00:26:58,761 --> 00:27:05,780
whose main purpose was to symbolize the futuristic jet age theme of the airport

334
00:27:05,781 --> 00:27:09,260
the overtly architectural restaurant and observation deck

335
00:27:09,261 --> 00:27:14,400
with its spindly legs reminiscent of an alien spacecraft, was named the theme building.

336
00:27:15,940 --> 00:27:21,960
When LAX opened, projections indicated that it would never need more than 10,000 parking spaces.

337
00:27:22,740 --> 00:27:26,080
They were off by at least a multiple of three.

338
00:27:27,060 --> 00:27:31,780
Anticipating future needs is one of the most important and most difficult tasks for airport officials.

339
00:27:32,820 --> 00:27:37,700
Looking at how the market may respond not just five years from now, but 20 years from now,

340
00:27:37,701 --> 00:27:43,420
It takes a tremendous amount of money and a lot of planning and environmental work to build a new hangar.

341
00:27:43,620 --> 00:27:49,560
In fact, these days, just to add capacity to an airport, it takes almost 10 years just to build a new runway.

342
00:27:51,340 --> 00:27:56,760
The volume of air traffic is increasing at such a rate that planners can barely keep up.

343
00:27:57,440 --> 00:28:04,900
Over 635 million people traveled by plane in 1999, up 22 million over the previous year.

344
00:28:04,901 --> 00:28:09,900
The importance of airport support facilities is greater than ever.

345
00:28:09,901 --> 00:28:14,900
Older airports must adapt or fail.

346
00:28:14,901 --> 00:28:23,900
At 53 square miles, Denver International Airport, or DIA, is one of the largest airports in the world.

347
00:28:23,901 --> 00:28:31,900
Completed in 1995, DIA is six times the size of LaGuardia and JFK airports combined.

348
00:28:31,901 --> 00:28:35,380
It is more than twice the size of Manhattan Island.

349
00:28:36,520 --> 00:28:40,340
In fact, the entire city of Boston could fit within the confines of DIA.

350
00:28:43,360 --> 00:28:49,780
Approximately 23 miles from downtown Denver, it handles over 104,000 passengers each day,

351
00:28:50,240 --> 00:28:57,540
with over 1,371 flights taking off and landing on its five nearly two-and-a-half-mile-long runways.

352
00:28:57,541 --> 00:29:06,120
One of the lines I heard that really defines that well is, how much metal can you pack in the sky?

353
00:29:07,200 --> 00:29:12,760
Constructing the massive airport took a roughly 10,000-person crew over five years.

354
00:29:13,740 --> 00:29:19,920
Situated on the plains of Colorado's eastern prairie, the task of leveling the land was an enormous job

355
00:29:19,921 --> 00:29:24,020
that encompassed shifting over 110 million cubic yards of earth.

356
00:29:24,021 --> 00:29:35,020
Runways were built in layers and over 2.5 million cubic yards of concrete were used to complete runways, taxiways and aprons.

357
00:29:35,021 --> 00:29:46,020
Denver's flight control tower at 327 feet, the tallest in North America, was designed with the latest Doppler radar innovations

358
00:29:46,021 --> 00:29:54,720
to evaluate weather conditions in an environment that sees violent tornado spawning thunderstorms and icy wind-shearing blizzards.

359
00:29:58,220 --> 00:30:04,060
DIA, like LaGuardia, divides loading and unloading passengers onto different floors to reduce congestion.

360
00:30:04,061 --> 00:30:11,060
Evoking the traveling lounges at Dulles, a light rail system at DIA takes passengers

361
00:30:11,061 --> 00:30:16,060
from their gates to the central terminal for picking up luggage and accessing ground transportation

362
00:30:16,061 --> 00:30:17,264
or parking lots.

363
00:30:17,265 --> 00:30:23,060
The central terminal is packed with so many retail outlets, the DIA resembles

364
00:30:23,061 --> 00:30:25,060
a giant shopping mall.

365
00:30:25,061 --> 00:30:27,813
It's sort of a mini city in itself.

366
00:30:27,814 --> 00:30:32,060
There are 25,000 plus employees at the Denver airport,

367
00:30:32,061 --> 00:30:37,800
for instance. And so there's a need for everything you can think of from nurse

368
00:30:37,801 --> 00:30:43,060
stations, a doctor, chapel, meeting spaces.

369
00:30:43,061 --> 00:30:50,060
DIA's architects not only made the airport gigantic, they went one step further and made it an icon.

370
00:30:50,061 --> 00:30:55,060
One of the most instantly recognizable airports ever built.

371
00:30:55,061 --> 00:30:59,681
We were very careful to try to incorporate something of this region.

372
00:30:59,682 --> 00:31:02,060
And so I searched long and hard for

373
00:31:02,061 --> 00:31:08,720
inspiration for something that said Colorado said the West and the thing

374
00:31:08,721 --> 00:31:13,280
that for me that kept being there was the mountains the mountains are very

375
00:31:13,281 --> 00:31:18,080
unique in this area of the country and so the sort of ridge shapes or peaks and

376
00:31:18,081 --> 00:31:23,420
valleys that we created with the roof there was inspired by the mountains and

377
00:31:23,421 --> 00:31:28,760
I think it kind of folds into the aspect of making buildings unique and

378
00:31:28,761 --> 00:31:34,700
different for the place. With an outer shell made of waterproof Teflon coated

379
00:31:34,701 --> 00:31:40,140
woven fiberglass, the roof membranes comprise over 15 acres of white peaks,

380
00:31:40,141 --> 00:31:46,400
which reflect 90% of the daylight. The remaining 10% of daylight sufficiently

381
00:31:46,401 --> 00:31:51,180
lights the interior of the terminal during the day. Amazingly, the roof

382
00:31:51,181 --> 00:31:55,940
membranes weigh less than two pounds per square foot and neither conduct nor

383
00:31:55,941 --> 00:32:02,720
restore heat. The airport cuts quite a profile alone out on the planes.

384
00:32:02,721 --> 00:32:09,020
Incredibly, despite its size and newness, DIA is already becoming inadequate.

385
00:32:09,021 --> 00:32:09,920
Some people

386
00:32:09,921 --> 00:32:14,760
complain that there isn't enough parking and efforts have been made to increase the facilities.

387
00:32:14,761 --> 00:32:19,060
Others complain that it's too far from the city and efforts are being made to design

388
00:32:19,061 --> 00:32:19,920
better access.

389
00:32:19,921 --> 00:32:24,220
Still others say it won't be able to handle increased traffic, up more

390
00:32:24,221 --> 00:32:27,370
than 20% since it opened its doors in 1995.

391
00:32:27,371 --> 00:32:30,520
Finally, some complain that there is always

392
00:32:30,521 --> 00:32:36,687
construction taking place. And not just at Denver, but practically everywhere.

393
00:32:36,688 --> 00:32:37,388
Airports

394
00:32:37,389 --> 00:32:38,729
never seem quite finished.

395
00:32:38,730 --> 00:32:42,360
One of the things that we have to keep in mind when we're designing

396
00:32:42,361 --> 00:32:47,000
an airport is that the life of the building is going to be over a long period of time,

397
00:32:47,380 --> 00:32:49,188
maybe 50 years, maybe longer.

398
00:32:49,189 --> 00:32:52,680
But over that 50-year period of time, we're going to see

399
00:32:52,681 --> 00:32:53,911
a lot of changes.

400
00:32:53,912 --> 00:32:58,980
And so to be able to create a building that has lots of flexibility in

401
00:32:58,981 --> 00:33:04,280
the future is paramount in the design of an airport.

402
00:33:04,281 --> 00:33:09,860
In Europe, expansion at 50-year-old Orly International Airport in Paris did not prevent the need

403
00:33:09,861 --> 00:33:17,240
for a brand new airport, which opened in 1974 as Charles de Gaulle International Airport.

404
00:33:17,241 --> 00:33:25,300
Anticipating jumbo jets in its design, de Gaulle nevertheless expanded in 1983, 1989, and again in 1994.

405
00:33:27,120 --> 00:33:32,840
Closed to commercial flights during World War II, London's Gatwick reopened in the 1950s

406
00:33:32,841 --> 00:33:37,300
to steady growth and expansion, despite continuing as a single runway airport.

407
00:33:38,700 --> 00:33:42,640
Today, Gatwick sees roughly 30 million passengers a year through its terminals.

408
00:33:42,641 --> 00:33:50,780
Amsterdam Schiphol, which saw renovations in the 1960s, 1980s and 1990s, also averages

409
00:33:50,781 --> 00:33:56,360
30 million passengers a year.

410
00:33:56,361 --> 00:34:03,060
In America, JFK's airport redevelopment, the largest ever, will cost over $9 billion and

411
00:34:03,061 --> 00:34:07,480
includes a light rail system linked to the city's subway and railroad.

412
00:34:07,481 --> 00:34:12,520
New and renovated terminals will spruce up the aging facility.

413
00:34:12,521 --> 00:34:22,320
Dulles enlarged its main terminal, opened new concourses, and added gates to handle traffic, which rose 26% between 1998 and 1999 alone.

414
00:34:23,340 --> 00:34:26,720
Dulles is the fastest growing of the world's 50 largest airports.

415
00:34:30,380 --> 00:34:37,500
LAX plans to improve with freeway and railway extensions, a people mover, a new terminal, and more parking.

416
00:34:37,501 --> 00:34:44,500
LAX, like all airports, is imperfect.

417
00:34:44,501 --> 00:34:51,500
If you hang the name of the city on the airport and the airport's a stinker, the city will take the rap.

418
00:34:51,501 --> 00:34:52,975
It can be out in the country.

419
00:34:52,976 --> 00:34:57,500
Sooner or later the city will show up around the airport and you'll have to deal with it.

420
00:34:57,501 --> 00:35:04,500
But from the get-go, the airport has to work. It has to be up and ready to roll.

421
00:35:04,501 --> 00:35:14,565
With all this activity going on, coordinating flights, travelers and cargo, managing noise, expansion and adaptability, you'd be surprised just how much your

422
00:35:14,566 --> 00:35:18,540
international airport is doing that you don't even know about.

423
00:35:20,120 --> 00:35:23,580
Welcome to McCarran International Airport in Las Vegas, Nevada.

424
00:35:24,700 --> 00:35:27,840
Don't forget to drop a quarter in the slot machine on your way to your luggage.

425
00:35:29,060 --> 00:35:33,760
When visiting London's Gatwick, be sure to grab some English tea biscuits as you leave the gate.

426
00:35:34,500 --> 00:35:39,500
Every airport reflects the character of its place.

427
00:35:39,501 --> 00:35:46,500
But beneath the surface, international airports function in roughly the same way, using roughly the same language.

428
00:35:46,501 --> 00:35:48,500
They have to.

429
00:35:48,501 --> 00:35:53,500
There's a kind of choreography, a ballet, that has to take place.

430
00:35:53,501 --> 00:35:59,500
And the speed at which that takes place determines the number of passengers that you're able to service.

431
00:35:59,501 --> 00:36:06,500
It's such an immensely complicated task to make this system work, but it's not simply making one system work,

432
00:36:06,501 --> 00:36:10,500
it's making hundreds of systems work all over the country.

433
00:36:10,501 --> 00:36:18,500
The system at LAX has to work in similar ways to the system at LaGuardia or National or Dulles.

434
00:36:18,501 --> 00:36:24,500
Those interconnected systems help guarantee that your travel will be safe and pleasant.

435
00:36:24,501 --> 00:36:28,500
It begins with security. Lots of security.

436
00:36:28,501 --> 00:36:34,000
Control rooms full of agents monitor television feeds from cameras all over the airport,

437
00:36:34,600 --> 00:36:37,220
from curbside check-in to terminal gate.

438
00:36:38,940 --> 00:36:43,860
At entry to gate area security, multiple cameras capture different angles

439
00:36:43,861 --> 00:36:47,240
as passengers unload their belongings for the search and scan.

440
00:36:48,400 --> 00:36:51,340
This security gauntlet began in the early 70s,

441
00:36:51,480 --> 00:36:55,800
after a series of hijacking incidents prompted the Federal Aviation Administration

442
00:36:55,801 --> 00:36:59,340
to require that all airports search travelers and their bags.

443
00:37:02,220 --> 00:37:06,020
Here at LAX, after a woman places her bags on the belt,

444
00:37:06,660 --> 00:37:09,340
a thief delays her from walking through the metal detector.

445
00:37:10,560 --> 00:37:14,920
On the other side, a different angle reveals his partner taking the woman's purse.

446
00:37:15,780 --> 00:37:18,520
This maneuver is known as a diversionary theft.

447
00:37:19,960 --> 00:37:22,180
Police were later able to retrieve the woman's purse

448
00:37:22,181 --> 00:37:25,660
by examining the security tape and identifying the suspect.

449
00:37:25,800 --> 00:37:35,960
High above the shops and restaurants, traffic control is managed within view of the runways.

450
00:37:35,961 --> 00:37:40,340
Controllers handle each of the planes that land every few seconds, every hour of every

451
00:37:40,341 --> 00:37:43,260
day of every year.

452
00:37:43,261 --> 00:37:46,920
Slips of paper represent every flight.

453
00:37:46,921 --> 00:37:51,200
Computers facilitate the communication between plane and tower, where the central controller

454
00:37:51,201 --> 00:37:54,160
gives the pilot his flight pattern.

455
00:37:54,161 --> 00:37:59,260
controller leads the plane out of the gate and onto the taxiway, and a third guides the

456
00:37:59,261 --> 00:38:00,340
plane into the air.

457
00:38:01,660 --> 00:38:05,431
They are absolutely the most valuable element at the airport.

458
00:38:05,432 --> 00:38:07,100
We operate 2,000 operations

459
00:38:07,101 --> 00:38:12,348
here. That's 1,000 airplanes landing, 1,000 airplanes taking off on a daily basis.

460
00:38:12,349 --> 00:38:13,049
The

461
00:38:13,050 --> 00:38:16,440
timing, the preciseness, the scheduling of all that, and their ability to manage that

462
00:38:16,441 --> 00:38:18,040
is really quite amazing.

463
00:38:19,420 --> 00:38:23,200
Down on the tarmac, rescue operators rehearse for disaster.

464
00:38:24,160 --> 00:38:26,660
Airports have to expect the unexpected.

465
00:38:27,640 --> 00:38:31,160
Their efforts make airline travel one of the safest modes of transportation.

466
00:38:34,220 --> 00:38:39,080
Millions of people travel through customs every year at any given international airport.

467
00:38:40,240 --> 00:38:45,020
While customs officials use X-ray equipment to look for illegal imports such as plants and animals,

468
00:38:45,520 --> 00:38:48,880
they also use dogs like this beagle to sniff out contraband.

469
00:38:48,881 --> 00:38:50,780
Find it, girl. Find it. Where is it?

470
00:38:50,900 --> 00:38:54,800
The dog taps suspect baggage with its paws to signal something suspicious.

471
00:38:55,220 --> 00:38:57,740
In this case, an orange from Turkey.

472
00:38:59,440 --> 00:39:02,320
This checkpoint makes agricultural inspectors happy,

473
00:39:02,460 --> 00:39:06,460
knowing that fruit or meat-borne toxins will find it difficult to get through the gauntlet.

474
00:39:07,320 --> 00:39:09,000
It makes the beagle happy, too.

475
00:39:09,700 --> 00:39:11,700
He gets a treat every time he finds something.

476
00:39:15,100 --> 00:39:17,920
Dozens of airlines plan and adjust their schedules

477
00:39:17,921 --> 00:39:21,940
while an army of mechanics check planes and conduct periodic tune-ups.

478
00:39:24,720 --> 00:39:30,240
Retail outlets bring in as much as $400 million in combined revenue at some airports.

479
00:39:30,620 --> 00:39:34,460
So much revenue that new airports today, like movie theaters,

480
00:39:34,720 --> 00:39:36,900
reap much of their profits from concessions.

481
00:39:37,900 --> 00:39:41,920
Some airports are focusing more on becoming a shopping mall

482
00:39:41,921 --> 00:39:45,040
than they are moving passengers in and out.

483
00:39:45,041 --> 00:39:49,080
and that's become a very important revenue source also for airports.

484
00:39:51,040 --> 00:39:54,880
Increasingly, airlines and airports are catering to non-human travelers.

485
00:39:56,160 --> 00:39:59,760
The biggest development at the airport is the air cargo business.

486
00:39:59,980 --> 00:40:01,580
We don't hear a lot about air cargo,

487
00:40:01,980 --> 00:40:07,080
but air cargo is the growing star in the transportation firmament.

488
00:40:08,680 --> 00:40:12,800
Perfectly successful airlines survive without ever boarding a paying customer.

489
00:40:13,800 --> 00:40:17,240
Instead, their planes are loaded with high-priority letters and packages,

490
00:40:17,880 --> 00:40:20,500
or maybe Blue Crab from Chesapeake Bay,

491
00:40:21,320 --> 00:40:24,400
or the latest passions from the runways of Paris and Milan.

492
00:40:26,860 --> 00:40:31,020
Even the bodies of the recently deceased travel in the bellies of airplanes.

493
00:40:33,280 --> 00:40:36,180
Computerized tracking allows cargo to clear through customs,

494
00:40:36,360 --> 00:40:38,840
often before the plane touches down in a new country.

495
00:40:38,841 --> 00:40:44,180
The newest airports have to work within this overarching system,

496
00:40:44,760 --> 00:40:47,400
even as they look for ways to improve on the past.

497
00:40:48,200 --> 00:40:50,140
This puts quite a burden on the architects,

498
00:40:50,400 --> 00:40:54,320
who design airports such as the new Incheon Airport and Seoul, South Korea.

499
00:40:56,160 --> 00:41:01,140
Right now, Korean Airlines is the second largest air cargo carrier in the world,

500
00:41:01,640 --> 00:41:07,000
and the development of Incheon will further move that number along,

501
00:41:07,001 --> 00:41:10,360
because the China market is everybody's goal,

502
00:41:11,080 --> 00:41:16,860
and Incheon will become an important trans gateway to the entire Asia-Pacific market.

503
00:41:19,020 --> 00:41:24,280
The airport is built on a man-made land bridge between two islands in the Yellow Sea,

504
00:41:24,720 --> 00:41:26,620
about 30 miles from downtown Seoul.

505
00:41:28,080 --> 00:41:31,300
The islands create a natural barrier to rough seas,

506
00:41:31,600 --> 00:41:34,420
which might otherwise make the location a forbidding site.

507
00:41:34,421 --> 00:41:39,420
The remote location also mitigates the effects of noise pollution.

508
00:41:39,421 --> 00:41:44,420
Pinterest Bradburn architects, who built Denver's airport,

509
00:41:44,421 --> 00:41:49,420
won a design competition to build the 5.6 million square foot central passenger terminal.

510
00:41:49,421 --> 00:41:52,420
They began with a model.

511
00:41:52,421 --> 00:41:58,420
In the design of buildings, we use models extensively for creation of the vision

512
00:41:58,421 --> 00:42:07,100
to help us illustrate and communicate to our clients and to the public at large what it is we're trying to do.

513
00:42:07,101 --> 00:42:12,680
And they're particularly interesting and helpful whenever we have something that is unique

514
00:42:12,681 --> 00:42:16,280
in terms of its spatial quality or the form of the building.

515
00:42:17,920 --> 00:42:23,700
The $6.9 billion airport includes 2.3 mile-long runways,

516
00:42:24,340 --> 00:42:25,620
two passenger terminals,

517
00:42:25,621 --> 00:42:31,500
and four remote concourses that will handle 27 million passengers a year.

518
00:42:33,640 --> 00:42:37,600
Inchon's control tower is 329 and a third feet high,

519
00:42:38,320 --> 00:42:40,200
three and a half feet taller than Denver's.

520
00:42:41,060 --> 00:42:44,540
The new airport is within three and a half hours flying time

521
00:42:44,541 --> 00:42:48,520
of 40 major metropolitan areas in the dynamic Pacific Rim.

522
00:42:50,400 --> 00:42:54,020
Undoubtedly, the airport's designers have left room for changes and adjustments

523
00:42:54,021 --> 00:42:59,260
for nobody really knows exactly where advancements in technology and engineering are going to lead.

524
00:43:00,960 --> 00:43:05,680
Amazingly, the next leap may be straight out of the atmosphere and into space.

525
00:43:06,180 --> 00:43:13,640
The Federal Aviation Administration already has as part of its mission the space program on the commercial side,

526
00:43:13,780 --> 00:43:19,380
and so we're looking at spaceports, incorporating that with runways and launch facilities.

527
00:43:19,381 --> 00:43:24,000
Someday we might be traveling to Mars and the airports will then become spaceports.

528
00:43:25,780 --> 00:43:29,680
International airports may someday become interplanetary ones.

529
00:43:30,580 --> 00:43:36,240
It's hard to imagine, but who could have predicted an airport like Incheon way back in 1939,

530
00:43:36,940 --> 00:43:39,120
when Mayor LaGuardia opened his airport in Queens?

531
00:43:39,760 --> 00:43:45,460
I think it's important for airport operators to maintain their sense of balance in terms of where they came from.

532
00:43:45,461 --> 00:43:52,160
I think that the old facilities, the classic terminals, are constant reminders of where we came from.

533
00:43:53,440 --> 00:43:57,280
As international airports build and rebuild themselves,

534
00:43:57,880 --> 00:44:01,800
their architects must constantly reflect and reinterpret those innovations

535
00:44:01,801 --> 00:44:06,680
which have made airports of the past successful, adaptable, and efficient.

536
00:44:07,780 --> 00:44:13,800
No longer modified train stations, international airports have evolved their own style,

537
00:44:13,801 --> 00:44:15,380
their own majesty.

538
00:44:16,420 --> 00:44:18,500
They are the first exotic destination

539
00:44:18,501 --> 00:44:20,440
on our far-flung journeys.

